kosha composites-varga

  -- materials made of two or more distinct components that together have
  -- properties neither component has alone.
  -- the two components: matrix (continuous phase) + reinforcement (dispersed phase).
  --
  -- examples and their components:
  --   concrete:    calcium silicate matrix + aggregate (sand, gravel)
  --                strong in compression, weak in tension.
  --   steel-reinforced concrete: concrete + steel rods.
  --                strong in both compression and tension.
  --   carbon-fibre composite: polymer matrix + carbon fibre.
  --                very strong, very light. aircraft, racing cars.
  --   wood:        cellulose fibres in lignin matrix.
  --                nature's composite — strong along grain, splits across.
  --   bone:        collagen (polymer) + hydroxyapatite (mineral).
  --                tough (collagen) + hard (mineral) — neither alone would work.
  --   alloy:       metal + metal (or metal + nonmetal).
  --                steel = Fe + C. brass = Cu + Zn. bronze = Cu + Sn.
  --
  -- alloys are a special case: components are mixed at the atomic level.
  -- the other composites have distinguishable microstructure.

